    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Overpayments are now kept as credit instead of being refused. Both acceptance tests pass with `bin/e2e`, before and after a final tidy-up of comments.\n\n**How payments work now** (all server-side, in `InvoiceService.java` and `Payment.java`):\n- **Payment against the open invoice:** the invoice gets what it still owes, up to the full amount. The audit records `PAYMENT_RECORDED` with the amount actually applied to the invoice, not the whole payment. If anything is left over, it stays on the client's account and `CREDIT_HELD payment=<id> amount=<rest>` is written. The rule that a part payment must be at least 10% of the invoice is now checked against the applied amount.\n- **Payment by reference(s) or applied automatically:** the invoices are paid in the same order as before, earliest due date first, and any remainder is kept as credit with the same `CREDIT_HELD` record. It is still refused if none of the target invoices owes anything; with nothing to pay against, it would really be a deposit.\n- **Split over the invoice boxes:** unchanged. The parts must add up exactly to the payment, and no box may be more than its invoice's amount due.\n\n**What already existed:** the app already treated any unapplied part of a payment as client credit. That credit is used up when the client's next invoice is sent, and it shows in the client's credit and balance figures. The payment-row and client-row `data-testid`s were already there too. So the front end only needed its comments updated where they still said overpayments are refused, and no database or test-data changes were needed.",
